From 075e18be19b8a5f2fcc5b782c4392169a4c0eec0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 02:13:05 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Video: a video site's mark, read from the page's keywords

YouTube took the layout of a 3D upload from the video's keywords (yt3d:enable=LR
and the others), and its watch page, like Invidious and Piped, still carries
them in <meta name="keywords">. A video whose stream and container carry no
stereo mark now takes that site mark, from libstereo-policy's site mark reader
(stereo_policy_site_mark_list, version 1.13), the one reader the edition's
programs share: the browser keeps no copy of the tags' rules.

A small actor (StereoSiteMark, browser/actors) hands the page's keywords to its
videos (HTMLVideoElement.stereoSiteKeywords, chrome only) when the video loads,
when the page's DOM is ready and when a single-page site announces new keywords
(YouTube's yt-navigate-finish; yt-navigate-start clears them). The content
process sends them to the parent (PContent.GetStereoSiteMark), which reads them
with the library, and applies the answer only if the element still has the same
keywords. The mark counts as the user's choice would for a video without a mark
of its own: under the user's own choice in the 3D Video menu and under the
stream's frame packing SEI and the container's mark, over the picture's shape.
Keywords that name no arrangement the policy has (an unsupported rectangle) say
so in the browser console and give no mark.

media.stereo.site-marks (on) switches it off; media.stereo.site-marks.hosts
(empty: every page) limits it to a list of domains.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 via Claude Code
